19 

High resolution STEM was used to study the distribution of sorbed cations on the GO sheets. The images collected using this method provide information about the distribution of Eu(III) on the BGO, HGO and TGO surfaces (Fig. 9). The white colour on Fig.9A,C,D,E corresponds to high atomic weight Eu atoms. In the case of BGO, Eu(III) is predominantly located on the wrinkles of the GO. In case of TGO and HGO, Eu is well-dispersed, with no preferred sorption on the edge sites of the GO. This is in agreement with the suggestions that Eu(III) and U(VI) interact with carboxyl groups located in holes and defects on the GO sheet. Homogeneous distribution of metal atoms over the surface of HGO and TGO flakes suggests that vacancy defects are an inherent property of GO produced by the permanganate route, in agreement with earlier studies[67]. 

